<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avarua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avarua is the capital of the Cook Islands, on the north coast of Rarotonga. It grew around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> station and the seat of the Makea ariki, whose leading title holder was regarded by Europeans as the island's monarch. It became the seat of the British resident in 1891 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> administration after 1901, and it is now the islands' main port and town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avarua.jpg" alt="Avarua" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: davehighbury · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Para_O_Tane_Palace,_Rarotonga,_Cook_Islands.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A resident commissioner at <b>Avarua</b> on Rarotonga governed on behalf of the New Zealand government.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands">Cook Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>After the London Missionary Society brought Christianity from 1823, through the Tahitian teacher Papeiha and John Williams, the ariki governed with mission backing, and the Makea title holders of <b>Avarua</b> came to be regarded by outsiders as monarchs of the island.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Kingdom of Rarotonga</a></li></ul></section>
